WEBVTT

NOTE
Captions for "Threads of Infinity — Leonhard Euler". Dialogue matches the UPDATED
micro-beat script (the corrected version). Timings sit on the ~10s beats and are
approximate — nudge any cue to match the finished cut. Speaker names shown for accessibility.

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.500
BERNOULLI: Leonard Euler was my friend — and the bravest question-asker I knew. Come with me to follow his thread.

2
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:29.000
BERNOULLI: In Basel I taught Euler the best tool in mathematics: a good question.

3
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.000
BERNOULLI: Every curve has its own voice, Leonhard. Listen, and it will tell you how it changes.

4
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.800
EULER: It rises, then falls … the slope sings where it bends. That's the sound of change.

5
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:49.500
BERNOULLI: A puzzle arrived from Königsberg — seven bridges no one could cross without repeating. Euler reimagined the problem.

6
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:59.500
EULER: If I mark each island as a point and each bridge as a line between them, the question becomes simple — it's not about walking paths, but connections.

7
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:09.500
BERNOULLI: Now Euler began to see bridges everywhere — even between geometry and numbers that stretched to infinity.

8
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:19.500
EULER: When I joined the infinite with the sine curve, they met in perfect harmony — and from that song came pi.

9
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:29.500
BERNOULLI: From curves that move to shapes that hold — Euler saw unity in change.

10
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:39.500
EULER: I turned the shapes, stretched them, squashed them — but their difference was always two. That number holds them all together.

11
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:49.500
BERNOULLI: In Berlin, Frederick demanded precision; in St. Petersburg, Catherine welcomed reason. Euler gave them both.

12
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.500
KING: Mathematics is fine, Euler.

13
00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.800
CATHERINE THE GREAT: But it must serve reason and rule.

14
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.000
EULER: Or imagination to lift hearts as well as armies.

15
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:06.500
BERNOULLI: Years of study dimmed his eyes, but not his vision. Even in darkness, Euler saw more clearly than most men ever will.

16
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.500
SCRIBE: Another theorem, Master?

17
00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:14.500
EULER: My eyes fade, but numbers shine within my mind.

18
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:23.000
BERNOULLI: Now the thread is yours. Ask a brave question — and pull.
